

Add the following:
# 1. Base image
FROM python:3.12-slim

# 2. Set working directory
WORKDIR /app

# 3. Install dependencies
COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# 4. Copy application source code
COPY app/ ./app/

# 5. Create non-root application user
RUN useradd --create-home --uid 10001 appuser \
    && chown -R appuser:appuser /app

# 6. Run application as non-root
USER appuser

# 7. Application port
EXPOSE 8000

# 8. Start FastAPI
CMD ["uvicorn", "app.main:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8000"]


Save using Ctrl + O, Enter, and Ctrl + X.
Understand each Dockerfile instruction
Instruction	Purpose
FROM	Selects the base image
WORKDIR	Sets application working directory
COPY	Copies files into the image
RUN	Executes commands during image building
USER	Specifies the runtime user
EXPOSE	Documents the application port
CMD	Defines the container startup command

Important: Why do we use 0.0.0.0 instead of 127.0.0.1?

Because the application must listen on the container's network interface so that requests forwarded from outside the container can reach it.
6. Step 4 – Create .dockerignore
We should not copy unnecessary files into the Docker image.

Create:
nano .dockerignore


Add:
venv/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
.git/
.env
*.pyc
tests/


Why?

Imagine your local virtual environment contains 500 MB of dependencies.
We don't need to copy that environment into the Docker image because our Dockerfile installs dependencies independently.

This reduces unnecessary image contents.
7. Step 5 – Build the Docker image
Now we are ready to build.

Run:
podman build -t enterprise-ai-platform:v1 .


Understand the command:
- podman build = Build an image.
- -t = Assign a name and tag.
- enterprise-ai-platform = Image name.
- v1 = Image version.
- . = Current directory is the build context.








petri.com


Check the image:
podman images


You should see something similar to:
REPOSITORY               TAG     IMAGE ID
localhost/enterprise-ai-platform  v1   abc123


Your image is now created. 8. Step 6 – Run the container Now let's run 
our application inside the container. podman run -d \
  --name ai-platform \
  -p 8000:8000 \
  enterprise-ai-platform:v1


Understand:
Option	Meaning
-d	Run in background
--name	Assign container name
-p 8000:8000	Map host port to container port
Image name	Image used to create container

The port mapping means:
Your RHEL machine
       |
       | localhost:8000
       |
       v
Container port 8000
       |
       v
FastAPI application


9. Step 7 – Test your containerized application
Check running containers:
podman ps


Check logs:
podman logs ai-platform


Test the API:
curl http://localhost:8000/


Test health:
curl http://localhost:8000/health


Open the API documentation:
http://localhost:8000/docs


Milestone 1 completed
Your application is now running inside a container rather than directly inside your RHEL Python environment.
10. Step 8 – Learn container management
These commands are important for a DevOps Engineer.
Command	Purpose
podman ps	Show running containers
podman ps -a	Show all containers
podman images	Show images
podman logs ai-platform	View application logs
podman inspect ai-platform	Inspect container configuration
podman stop ai-platform	Stop container
podman start ai-platform	Start stopped container
podman restart ai-platform	Restart container
podman rm ai-platform	Remove stopped container
podman rmi IMAGE	Remove image

Try these commands yourself. 11. Step 9 – Understand environment 
variables Production applications shouldn't hardcode configuration 
values.
For example, imagine your application needs:
DATABASE_URL
API_KEY
ENVIRONMENT


We can pass environment variables when starting a container:
podman run -d \
  --name ai-platform \
  -p 8000:8000 \
  -e ENVIRONMENT=production \
  enterprise-ai-platform:v1


Check:
podman exec ai-platform printenv ENVIRONMENT


Security note
Do not pass real production passwords or API keys directly in shell commands or commit them to Git. Later, we'll implement secure secrets management using Azure Key Vault.
12. Step 10 – Understand Docker Compose
Imagine your application has three components:
Frontend
Backend
Database


Running each container separately becomes difficult.

Docker Compose allows you to define multiple containers in one YAML file.

Create:
nano compose.yaml


Add:
services:
  ai-api:
    build:
      context: .
    container_name: ai-platform
    ports:
      - "8000:8000"
    restart: unless-stopped


On RHEL, you can use Podman Compose as a compatible Compose implementation. If installed, run:
podman-compose up -d --build


For now, our project has only one service, so Compose is mainly an introduction. We will expand multi-service deployment later if needed.

